  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how stem-cell therapies for heart repair have evolved from “first-generation” to “next-generation” approaches. In the top part, cells are collected from an iliac crest bone marrow aspirate, producing a mixed population of bone marrow mononuclear cells. These are then separated or expanded into two broad cell types: endothelial progenitor cells and mesenchymal stem cells. This presents the earlier strategy as relying on relatively general adult stem or progenitor cells taken from bone marrow.\n\nThe lower part shows newer, more specialized cell sources. One route starts from resident cardiac stem cells already present in heart tissue, which can be isolated directly or grown into cardiospheres. A second route still begins with bone marrow from the iliac crest, but the harvested cells are then treated with a “cardiopoietic” growth factor cocktail to drive lineage specification, yielding cardiopoietic stem cells. \n\nOverall, the comparison emphasizes a shift from using broadly defined bone marrow-derived cell populations toward more targeted, lineage-guided or heart-derived stem cells that appear intended to be more specifically suited for cardiac regeneration.",
